T*
(structured peer-facilitated counselling could enable PLHIV to become community prevention) Tj
T*
(advocates. Methods: Two hundred ten PLHIV in Uganda were randomized to an 8-module peer-) Tj
T*
(led behaviour intervention or control. Participants completed assessments at baseline, 6,) Tj
T*
(12, and 18 months. Primary outcomes: internalized stigma \(assessed via 8-item standardized) Tj
T*
(instrument\) and prevention advocacy behaviour. Secondary outcomes: disclosure to social) Tj
T*
(network members; among 599 network members, HIV testing uptake and condom use frequency.) Tj
T*
(Results: The intervention group reported significantly lower internalized stigma compared) Tj
T*
(with control at 18 months \(p=0.002\). Self-reported prevention advocacy activities were) Tj
T*
(higher in intervention participants \(mean 12.4 versus 6.8 activities in past year;) Tj
T*
(p<0.001\). Advocacy self-efficacy increased by 37% in intervention versus 8% in controls) Tj
T*
(\(p<0.001\). Among social network members, those aware of a network member's HIV advocacy) Tj
T*
(efforts showed higher HIV testing rates \(58% versus 42%; p=0.008\) and reported more) Tj
